WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.920
Good Morning Hank, it's Tuesday.

00:00:00.920 --> 00:00:04.500
Today I'll be reviewing the mobile version of the famous board game Monopoly.

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:07.560
I hadn't played Monopoly in a number of years before downloading the app, and

00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:09.920
what I'd forgotten (or maybe just never noticed)

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:12.480
is that Monopoly is a terrible game.

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:16.520
The basic idea you buy property and then charge rent when your opponent lands on that property

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:21.220
leads to a game that is distressingly similar to real contemporary human life.

00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:23.940
Monopoly unfolds slowly at first and very quickly at the end,

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:26.740
people find meanings in its outcomes even though it's mostly random,

00:00:26.740 --> 00:00:28.600
and it's only occasionally fun.

00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:31.280
The mobile game brings all the terrible things about Monopoly

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:33.960
to your phone, while adding some new additional terrible things.

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:36.180
Consider for instance Rich Uncle Penny Bags,

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:37.960
who plays a minor role in the board game

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:39.220
but here shows up to introduce

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:42.140
you to Monopoly EVERY SINGLE TIME YOU PLAY.

00:00:42.140 --> 00:00:44.220
Also, since you can't play online with friends or

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:45.840
enemies, you're forced to play against a not

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:48.260
particularly intelligent Artificial Intelligence.

00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:50.980
And when the AI begins to lose, it quickly becomes desperate,

00:00:50.980 --> 00:00:53.880
proposing on each turn increasingly ludicrous trades

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:56.080
in an attempt to acquire properties, and you have to

00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:58.160
decline these trades over and over, and after a while

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:00.380
you start to feel guilty because the ONLY thing

00:01:00.380 --> 00:01:02.740
this Artificial Intelligence has been told to do is

00:01:02.740 --> 00:01:05.220
win at Monopoly, and so now it's failing at

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:07.460
literally its only goal, whereas

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:09.720
you, a thinking feeling human, have a variety of

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:12.020
passions and fulfillments outside this mobile game

00:01:12.020 --> 00:01:14.480
so sometimes you just end up agreeing to the ridiculous trades

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:17.520
to make the AI happy, even so, you usually win eventually.

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:19.280
but you can't even enjoy it properly because

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:22.580
you feel so sorry for the sad little Artificial Intelligence kitten.

00:01:22.580 --> 00:01:23.940
Or maybe that's just me.

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:25.800
Anyway, by far the worst thing about Monopoly,

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:27.800
both the board game and the mobile app, is its

00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:31.040
convoluted self-contradictory analysis of capitalism.

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:33.200
Like, the game is essentially about how acquiring

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:35.940
land and the ability to exploit monopolies is literally

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:36.840
a roll of the dice,

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:38.560
and yet the game also celebrates getting rich.

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:41.840
I mean the only way to win is to bankrupt your opponents,

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:44.900
and this turns out to be partly a product of Monopoly's

00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:48.520
complicated origin story. So in 1935 a door-to-door salesman

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:51.080
named Charles Darrow sold his board game Monopoly

00:01:51.080 --> 00:01:52.560
to the company Parker Brothers.

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:54.780
The game became a hugely successful; Darrow was the

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:56.180
first board game millionaire,

00:01:56.180 --> 00:01:59.080
and his story embodied the American Dream, a rags

00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:02.300
to riches tale of an american inventor who became successful

00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:04.760
via his individual genius and hard work.

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:07.360
Today there's a plaque celebrating Darrow on the boardwalk

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:09.840
at Atlantic City, and Parker Brothers often prints his

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:12.380
heroic story alongside the game's instructions.

00:02:12.380 --> 00:02:14.880
There's just one problem, which is that Charles Darrow

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:16.100
did not invent Monopoly.

00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:18.920
30 years earlier, a woman named Elizabeth Magie

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:21.280
invented a board game called The Landlord's Game,

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:22.680
and although it was in many ways similar

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:25.380
to the Monopoly we play today, it was a more direct

00:02:25.380 --> 00:02:28.480
attack on land monopolism and an exploration of how over time,

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:32.800
capital tends to become concentrated in fewer and fewer hands.

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:35.360
She patented the game, and even tried to sell it to Parker Brothers,

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:38.320
but they passed. Still, the Landlord's game became popular

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:40.740
in progressive circles and was even used as a teaching tool

00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:43.300
at the University of Pennsylvania's Wharton Finance school.

00:02:43.300 --> 00:02:46.040
For decades, many people played the game and

00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:48.220
changed the rules as they went. Along the way, some

00:02:48.220 --> 00:02:50.900
versions of it came to be called Monopoly, and then in

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:54.620
1933, Charles Darrow played Monopoly at a friend's house

00:02:54.620 --> 00:02:57.700
and afterwards, asked for a written version of the rules.

00:02:57.700 --> 00:03:01.160
He altered some of the design, patented the game, and became a millionaire.

00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:03.360
And so the story we hear of an individual

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:07.320
rightly rewarded for his genius, turns out to be a far more complicated

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:10.160
story of a woman who invented a game that thousands of

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:12.560
collaborators then improved by playing it.

00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:16.900
A story of capitalism working, turns out to be a story of capitalism failing.

00:03:16.900 --> 00:03:19.560
I mean, the game that became so popular was really created

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:22.100
by a community,  and the free market failed to reward that

00:03:22.100 --> 00:03:26.160
community, instead wrongly assigning most of the value to one contributor who,

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:29.300
truthfully, added very little to the game. This iPhone app,

00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:32.000
by the way, is published by Hasbro, the multi-national

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:33.960
conglomerate that now owns the very same

00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:36.760
Parker Brothers company that declined Elizabeth Magie's game,

00:03:36.760 --> 00:03:39.200
only to later buy Charles Darrow's version.

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:41.260
Of course, none of this answers the underlying question,

00:03:41.260 --> 00:03:44.240
which is how anyone ever enjoyed Monopoly in the first place.

00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:46.620
(I guess maybe  Settlers of Catan hadn't been invented yet.)

00:03:46.620 --> 00:03:48.760
Regardless, the history of Monopoly turns out

00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:51.740
to be a far more interesting study of capitalism than the

00:03:51.740 --> 00:03:55.540
boring game itself. (I give it 2 out of 10, not recommended.)

00:03:55.540 --> 00:03:57.120
Hank, I'll see you on Friday.

